    <dashboard name='Predicting default' type='storyboard'>
      <layout-options>
        <title>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontname='Tableau Bold' fontsize='16'>Who Pays Back? Predicting Default and Cancellation with Logistic Regression</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </title>
      </layout-options>
      <style>
        <style-rule element='story-description'>
          <format attr='background-color' id='story-description_8' value='#ffffffd9' />
          <format attr='background-color' id='story-description_7' value='#ffffffd9' />
          <format attr='background-color' id='story-description_6' value='#ffffffd9' />
          <format attr='border-color' id='story-description_6' value='#666666' />
          <format attr='border-color' id='story-description_7' value='#666666' />
          <format attr='border-color' id='story-description_8' value='#555555' />
        </style-rule>
      </style>
      <size maxheight='1000' maxwidth='1600' minheight='1000' minwidth='1600' preset-index='17' sizing-mode='fixed' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='2' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone h='98400' id='1' param='vert' removable='false' type-v2='layout-flow' w='99000' x='500' y='800'>
            <zone h='2900' id='3' type-v2='title' w='99000' x='500' y='800' />
            <zone h='10100' id='4' is-fixed='true' paired-zone-id='5' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard-nav' w='99000' x='500' y='3700' />
            <zone h='85400' id='5' paired-zone-id='4' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard' w='99000' x='500' y='13800'>
              <flipboard active-id='2' nav-type='caption' show-nav-arrows='true'>
                <story-points>
                  <story-point caption='Introduction' captured-sheet='' id='1' />
                  <story-point caption='The model separates risky from safe loans (AUC 0.70)' captured-sheet='Model Results: default' id='2' />
                  <story-point caption=' The riskiest 10% default 24.5%, 14× more than the safest 10%' captured-sheet='Risk groups: default' id='3' />
                  <story-point caption='Rating and country drive default, even with all else equal' captured-sheet='Odds ratios: default' id='4' />
                  <story-point caption='The cut-off is a business choice: more caught means more false alarms' captured-sheet='Cut-off defaults' id='5' />
                  <story-point caption='3,131 young loans are already in the riskiest group' captured-sheet='Early warning: young loans' id='6' />
                  <story-point caption='Cancellation is harder to predict (AUC 0.65)' captured-sheet='Model Results: cancellation' id='7' />
                  <story-point caption='Cancellation depends on country and loan size, not on the rating' captured-sheet='Odds ratios: cancellation' id='8' />
                  <story-point caption='Recommendations' captured-sheet='' id='9' />
                  <story-point caption='Limitations' captured-sheet='' id='10' />
                </story-points>
              </flipboard>
            </zone>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#444444' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone flipboard-zone-id='5' forceUpdate='true' h='52500' hidden='true' id='6' story-point-id='1' type-v2='text' w='61875' x='8500' y='18000'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Can we tell, on the day a loan is issued, who will default?</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Goal: predict two yes/no outcomes for each loan:</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Æ  </run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Default within 12 months (9.1% of loans)</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Cancellation (4.1% of loans)</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Method: multiple logistic regression</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Æ  </run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Inputs known at issue: income, loan amount, interest rate, duration, country, risk rating.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Trained on 308,319 loans, tested on 77,080 loans it had never seen.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Linear regression was tried first, but it gave impossible negative probabilities, so logistic regression was used.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Note: odds ratios are compared with Denmark and rating A.</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </zone>
        <zone flipboard-zone-id='5' forceUpdate='true' h='57100' hidden='true' id='7' story-point-id='9' type-v2='text' w='60438' x='31250' y='17100'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Recommendations</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>1. Use the model score as a screening step. Review the riskiest 10–20% of new loans by hand:      the riskiest 20% contain 42% of all defaults.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>2. Monitor young loans now, not after 12 months. 3,131 loans from late 2025 are already in the riskiest group: contact or watch them before they default.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>3. Set the cut-off by review capacity. Cut-off 0.5 flags 41% of loans and catches 68% of defaults.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Cut-off 0.7 flags only 5%, but 30% of those really default.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>4. Keep rating and country at the centre of approval. A G-rated loan has 3.4× the default odds of an A-rated loan; Denmark and Spain are the riskiest countries.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>5. Use the cancellation model for planning, not for approval. It is weaker (AUC 0.65); cancellations come mainly from Estonia, Latvia and Finland and from smaller, shorter loans.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>6. Retrain the model every year and add more borrower data(e.g. employment, existing debts) to make it stronger.</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </zone>
        <zone flipboard-zone-id='5' forceUpdate='true' h='53700' hidden='true' id='8' story-point-id='10' type-v2='text' w='45750' x='39625' y='17300'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>Limitations</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>1. Only 6 inputs: the models are fair (AUC 0.70 and 0.65), not strong, and raise many false alarms (precision 15% and 6%).</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>2. The scores rank loans; they are not exact probabilities (a score of 0.5 does not mean a 50% chance of default).</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>3. Only default within 12 months was modelled. 25,466 young loans had no 12-month outcome yet and were left out of training.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>4. Association, not cause. Small groups (Spain, rating HR with 19 loans) give less stable results.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>5. Logistic regression treats each input separately; combinations (e.g. Latvia × rating) are not captured. A tree-based model could do better.</run>
            <run fontalignment='0'>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            <run bold='true' fontalignment='0' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='16'>6. Trained on Bondora peer-to-peer loans (2023–2025); results may change over time and do not transfer directly to banks.</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <simple-id uuid='{E58B24E0-CBF1-4EAD-84EC-E1864FD84BE0}' />
    </dashboard>
